findings.body:
The `automation_triggered_event_id` validator requires `triggering_event` to be non-None whenever the ID is set. But `act()` always sets the ID to the Triggered state-change event whenever one exists, and proactive firings can have no triggering event: `Firing(triggering_event=triggering_event or bucket.last_event)` in `triggers.py` around line 195, and absence-style buckets are created with `last_event=None` (around line 1236). When that happens, `TriggeredAction(...)` raises `ValidationError` inside `act()`. The state-change events were already published, but no action message is published. The exception then propagates out of `fire()`, so the bucket cleanup after `fire()` is skipped and the proactive trigger will likely keep re-firing on later evaluations.

The validator's invariant is also wrong. The `automation.triggered` event is a valid link target regardless of whether the action has a reactive triggering event. The existing test `test_acting_publishes_an_action_message_from_a_proactive_trigger` (triggering_event=None) exercises exactly this path and will fail.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/schemas/automations.py" lines="772-780"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The validator's docstring says the ID should be set "only when triggering_event exists". That contradicts how `act()` uses it, so the fix is to remove the validator rather than adjust it.

If you want to keep some guard, validate that the ID is a UUID only, and leave the `triggering_event` relationship alone.

In `Action.fail()`, the `prefect.automation.action.triggered` event is now emitted with `related=self._resulting_related_resources` (line 202), not with `related_resources`. The `related_resources` list is the one that has the `automation-triggered-event` and `triggering-event` links. Because of this, `action.triggered` emitted on failure has none of the trace links, while the `action.failed` event right below it still has them. `succeed()` still passes `related_resources` to its triggered event, so the two paths now behave differently.

This looks like an accidental swap of the two `related=` lines in the diff. The new tests `test_failure_events_include_automation_triggered_event_link` and `test_action_events_without_automation_triggered_event_id` assert that `triggered_event.related` contains these links, so they will fail. The PR description and docs both say the links go on the action events.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="196-216"}

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="202-202"}
                    related=related_resources,

The same block that builds the `automation-triggered-event` and `triggering-event` related resources now appears verbatim in both `fail()` and `succeed()`. That duplication is how the `fail()` swap slipped through: the two copies can drift. A small helper such as `_build_related_resources(triggered_action)` would keep the two paths in sync and make the `related=` choice explicit.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/actions.py" lines="176-192"}

The `resource` reuse in `succeed()` is a good cleanup, but the related-resource logic should get the same treatment.

`act()` gives every action the same `primary_state_change_event_id`, which prefers the Triggered event over Resolved. For `actions_on_resolve` entries, however, the action's own `triggering_event` is the Resolved state-change event (`resolved_event`). If a single firing contains both states, those actions will point their `automation-triggered-event` link at the `automation.triggered` event, even though they were prompted by `automation.resolved`. The comment and the docs say the link should point to the event that prompted the action.

It's an edge case (both states in one firing may be rare), but it's a correctness mismatch with the documented contract. Use the state-change event that matches each action's source state.

::diff{file="src/prefect/server/events/triggers.py" lines="459-476"}
